# wrapped_retain_release.tkr — the COL-F0c wrapped-refcount fixture (crumb 0023). `main.tks`
# creates one object as its own region (`region_new(region_root())`), retains it twice, releases it
# once — the count drops from 2 to 1 and the object must still be alive, proven by a fresh
# `region_new` NOT recycling the object's header — then releases it a second time, driving the count
# to zero, proven this time by a fresh `region_new` recycling the EXACT SAME header address (the
# LIFO header free-list only returns an address once it has actually been freed). Exits 0 only if
# the object survives the first release and is freed exactly at the second. The compiler holds no
# `wrapped` objects, so this path is not fixpoint-exercised. Pinned to the C backend: `retain`/
# `release` emit on the C route only.

Feature: wrapped_retain_release — retain/release free a wrapped object exactly at zero, never before

  Scenario: retained_twice_released_twice_frees_exactly_at_the_second_release
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
